<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernst Glück</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1654">1654</a>–<a href="/years/1705">1705</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernst Glück was a German Lutheran pastor in Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> who translated the Bible into Latvian, published in 1685–1694, and worked on an Estonian translation. Based at Marienburg (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aluksne">Alūksne</a>), he also founded schools for Latvian peasants. Captured by the Russians in 1702, he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where he ran a school; his ward Marta Skavronska later became Empress Catherine I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernst-gluck.jpg" alt="Ernst Glück" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M_H.DE · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wettin,Gedenktafel_Johann_Ernst_Gl%C3%BCck.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
